After receipt of the application and all other prerequisites as herein set out, the clerk shall consider the application and, if all criteria have been satisfied, shall issue the license applied for. If, however, the clerk, upon consideration, finds that the application fails to satisfy any of the requirements for issuance of a license, the clerk shall take no action at such time, but shall notify the applicant in writing, mailed to the address given in the application; or shall have the written notification personally delivered to the applicant with an affidavit of service returned to the clerk so that such notification shall be received at least three days prior to a day and time certain set for a hearing before the full council. Said notice shall state:
(1) Specifically the time, date and place for the hearing;
(2) Complete statement of the requirements which have not been satisfied and upon which the council proposes to take action;
(3) That the applicant must appear in person and has the right to be accompanied by an attorney;
(4) The names of any witnesses at the time known who may be called by the council and that the applicant shall have the right to confront and cross examine such witnesses;
(5) That the applicant has the right to call any witnesses in the applicant's behalf who may be examined by the council, and that all testimony and evidence shall be introduced and given under oath and shall be recorded.
If the applicant fails to appear and has not been excused or granted an extension of time, the application shall be denied. If the applicant appears and fails to satisfy the requirements of this article as stated in the written notice, the application shall be denied. If the applicant appears and all requirements are satisfied, the application shall be granted.
(Code 1986, § 4-15)

124:
This article is enacted for the purpose of:
(1) Establishing minimum standards governing the use, occupancy, condition, and maintenance of property, dwellings, buildings, and structures;
(2) Establishing minimum standards governing supplied utilities and facilities and other physical things and conditions essential to make dwellings, buildings, structures, and surrounding premises safe, sanitary, and fit for human use and habitation;
(3) Promoting and protecting the public health and safety through the control of weeds and grass which constitute a fire hazard;
(4) Fixing certain responsibilities and duties of owners, operators, and occupants of dwellings;
(5) Authorizing the inspection of dwellings and structures;
(6) Providing for the condemnation of all buildings and structures deemed unfit for human use and habitation and providing for removal of such buildings at public expense after hearing; and
(7) For other purposes.

146:
(a) Dangerous or injurious conditions. The public officer may determine, under existing regulations, that a dwelling, building, or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial, or business use if he finds that conditions exist in such building, dwelling, or structure which are dangerous or injurious to the health, safety, or morals of the occupants of such dwelling, building, or structure; of the occupants of neighborhood dwellings, buildings, or structures; or of other residents of the city. Such conditions include the following (without limiting the generality of the foregoing):
(1) Defects therein increasing the hazards of fire, accidents, or other calamities;
(2) Lack of adequate ventilation, light, or sanitary facilities;
(3) Dilapidation;
(4) Disrepair;
(5) Structural defects;
(6) Uncleanness;
(7) The existence of other conditions of the premises which are in violation of this article or other relevant regulations of the city, such as, without limitation, the existence of weeds, junk, garbage, and trash on the premises which affect the health and/or safety of the property and/or the occupants of the premises and/or other persons residing in the city;
(8) Other additional standards which may from time to time be adopted and referenced herein by ordinance amendment.
(b) Use of structure for drug crimes. The public officer may determine, under existing regulations, that a dwelling, building, or structure is vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes based upon personal observation or report of a law enforcement agency and evidence of drug crimes being committed.

148:
The public officers designated in this article shall have the following powers:
(1) Investigation. To investigate the dwelling conditions in the city in order to determine which dwellings, buildings, or structures therein are unfit for human habitation or are unfit for current commercial, industrial, or business use or are vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes;
(2) Administration of oaths, examination of witnesses, receipt of evidence. To administer oaths and affirmations, to examine witnesses, and to receive evidence;
(3) Entry to premises. To enter upon premises for the purpose of making examinations; provided, however, that such entries shall be made in such manner as to cause the least possible inconvenience to the persons in possession;
(4) Delegation. To delegate any of their functions and powers under this article to such officers and agents as may be provided by the city; and
(5) Procedures to enforce and abate authorized. To utilize the in rem enforcement and abatement procedures, including the lis pendens, the issuance of liens and the recording and collection of same, provided by this article in all cases where there exist on the property junk, weeds, grass, junked or abandoned appliances, junked or abandoned automobiles, garbage, trash, or any other conditions which are in noncompliance with or in violation of this article, or any other applicable ordinances, codes, or laws; and the provisions of said sections shall apply fully to the abatement of such conditions.

183:
All development activities or site work conducted after approval of the site plan shall conform with the specifications of said site plan. Significant changes to the site plan that would:
(1) Alter the amount and velocity of stormwater runoff from the site;
(2) Increase the amount of impervious surface within the development;
(3) Alter the overall density of development;
(4) Result in a considerable increase in the amount of excavation;
(5) Fill or remove vegetation during construction; or
(6) Otherwise result in an alteration of the overall appearance of the development as proposed;
can be amended only with the approval of the permitting officer. Minor changes, such as the realignment of streets or minor alterations to drainage structures and other infrastructure to meet unexpected conditions, are exempted from this requirement.
(Ord. of 6-4-2001(1), § 4.3)

189:
(a) Wetlands are indispensable and fragile natural resources with significant development constraints due to flooding, erosion and soils limitations. In its natural state, wetlands serve man and nature. They provide:
(1) Habitat for fish, wildlife, and vegetation;
(2) Water quality maintenance and pollution control;
(3) Flood control;
(4) Erosion control;
(5) Natural resource education;
(6) Scientific study; and
(7) Open space and recreational opportunities.
In addition, the wise management of forested wetlands is essential to the economic well-being of many communities within the State of Georgia.
(b) Nationally, a considerable number of wetland resources have been lost or impaired by draining, dredging, filling, excavating, building, pollution and other acts. Damaging or destroying wetlands threatens public safety and the general welfare. It is, therefore, necessary to discourage development activities that may adversely affect wetlands by ensuring maximum protection for wetlands located in the corporate limits of the city.
(Ord. of 6-4-2001(2), § 1.1)

